        <div id="qi2-explained" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Qi2, Qi2.2 and MagSafe: What the Labels Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            MagSafe is Apple’s name for the magnet ring and charging coil on the back of most iPhones since the iPhone 12. For years, only Apple’s own chargers and a handful of licensed products could charge through it at full speed. Third-party banks that simply added a ring of magnets were sold as “MagSafe compatible,” and most of them charged at the old Qi rate of 7.5W on an iPhone, sometimes less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Qi2 changed that. The Wireless Power Consortium built Apple’s magnetic alignment into the open Qi standard, and a Qi2-certified bank can charge a compatible iPhone at up to 15W. Certification also means the magnet ring and coil are where they should be, which affects both hold and efficiency: a coil that sits a few millimetres off-center wastes more power as heat. That is why every bank on this page carries Qi2 or newer, and why we would not buy one that does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Qi2.2, sometimes marketed as Qi2 25W, raises the ceiling to 25W. The iPhone 17 line supports it, and Apple enabled 25W Qi2.2 charging on most iPhone 16 models with iOS 26; reporting at the time noted the iPhone 16e was left out. Older iPhones and most Android phones still charge at 15W or less on a Qi2.2 bank, so the faster standard does not hurt them but does not help either. Baseus spells this out on its own listing, which caps Qi2 and Qi phones at 15W and some Pixel models lower.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Qi2 is the floor. Qi2.2 is worth it only for an iPhone 16 or 17.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A non-certified “MagSafe compatible” bank usually charges an iPhone at 7.5W or less. Qi2 doubles that to 15W. Qi2.2 adds up to 25W, but only phones that support it see the gain.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            What about Apple’s own MagSafe Battery Pack? Apple discontinued it, and while you will still find it secondhand, there is no reason to seek one out. Its capacity was small, it was not a Qi2 product, and every bank on this page outdoes it on capacity, wired output, or both. If you see it on a listing, treat it as a collectible, not a recommendation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Android owners can use most of these banks too, with a caveat. Qi2 magnets only help if your phone or case has a magnet ring. Several recent Android phones ship with Qi2 magnets built in; for the rest, a Qi2 magnetic case solves it. Without magnets the bank will still charge if you hold it in place, but you lose the whole point of a magnetic battery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What You Actually Get From 10,000mAh
          </h2>

          <p>
            Capacity on a power bank box is measured at the internal cell voltage, usually around 3.7 to 3.85 volts. Anker lists the MagGo 10K at 38.5Wh, which is 10,000mAh at 3.85V. Your phone does not receive 38.5Wh. The bank has to step the voltage up to USB levels, the phone has to step it back down to charge its own battery, and wireless charging adds a coil-to-coil transfer that loses more energy as heat than a cable does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Baseus is unusually candid about the result. Its own FAQ for the PicoGo AM52 says roughly 5,500 to 6,500mAh of the rated 10,000mAh is usable after losses. That is a fair working figure for any 10K magnetic bank used wirelessly, and it lines up with the general rule in our <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power bank guide</a>, which estimates that most banks deliver around 60 to 70 percent of their rated energy at the port over a cable. Wireless lands toward the lower end or below it.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practical terms, a 10K magnetic bank gets a current standard-size iPhone from low to roughly full once, with a little to spare if you plug in instead of snapping on. A Pro Max-size phone with a bigger battery gets less than a full charge. A 5K bank like the Anker Nano Slim is a top-up: enough to rescue an afternoon, not to carry you through a weekend. If you need several full charges, a magnetic bank is the wrong tool; look at a 20,000mAh cable bank instead, and if you need to run a laptop too, our <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">laptop power bank guide</a> covers banks with 65W and 100W USB-C.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Capacity Math</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">38.5Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Anker MagGo 10K rated energy</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5.5-6.5K</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usable mAh Baseus cites for its 10K</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Qi2 wireless ceiling</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">25W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Qi2.2 ceiling on supported iPhones</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            There are two habits that recover a lot of the lost energy. First, use the cable when you can. A USB-C cable from the bank to the phone avoids the coil loss entirely, and every bank here has a USB-C port for exactly this reason. Second, do not leave the bank attached once the phone is full. Some banks keep feeding the phone to hold it at 100 percent, which burns capacity on nothing. Snap it on when you need charge, take it off when you have it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Buying Guide: The Specs That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beyond certification and capacity, five specs separate a magnetic bank you will love from one that ends up in a drawer. In rough order of importance they are the case you use, the stand, the wired port, the weight and thickness, and the indicator.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Your case comes first</h3>

          <p>
            Magnetic banks attach through the case, not around it. A MagSafe-compatible case with its own magnet ring gives the strongest hold and best coil alignment. A thick non-magnetic case can cut the hold to the point that the bank slides off in a pocket, and the extra distance between coils increases heat and lowers charging speed. If the bank you buy seems weak, check the case before blaming the bank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A kickstand is worth more than it looks</h3>

          <p>
            Three of our five picks have one. A stand turns the bank into a desk stand, a nightstand charger, or a way to watch video on a plane tray without propping the phone against a cup. The Anker MagGo 10K uses a fold-out stand, the Belkin and ESR use an integrated kickstand. If you mostly charge in a pocket, skip it and save the weight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wired output is your fast lane</h3>

          <p>
            Every bank here has USB-C, and you should use it more than you think. A 20W or faster USB-C port charges an iPhone faster and more efficiently than 15W wireless, and it lets the bank top up earbuds, a watch charger or a second phone. If you carry an iPad or a thin laptop, the 45W port on the Baseus AM52 is the only one here with real headroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight and thickness</h3>

          <p>
            A 10K magnetic bank typically weighs between about 200 and 250 g, which you feel on the back of a phone. The 5K class is closer to half that. Thickness matters more than weight for pocket carry: the Anker Nano 5K Slim is 8.6 mm, while the full-feature Anker MagGo 10K is just under 20 mm with its stand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">An honest indicator</h3>

          <p>
            A percentage display, like the one on the Anker MagGo 10K, tells you whether you have enough for the evening. Four LEDs only tell you that you have “some.” It is a small thing until you leave home with a bank you thought was full.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Heat, Battery Health and Charging Habits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wireless charging makes heat. The phone gets warm, the bank gets warm, and heat is what lithium batteries like least. iPhones protect themselves by slowing charging when they get hot, which is why a magnetic bank on a phone in a sunny car or a tight pocket sometimes seems to stall. That is not a fault in the bank; it is the phone throttling to protect its own battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few habits keep both batteries healthier. Take the phone out of a thick case if it gets hot while charging. Avoid charging wirelessly while gaming or navigating with the screen at full brightness, which stacks the phone’s own heat on top of the charging heat. Do not leave a bank on the dashboard or in a hot car; our <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">guide to solar power banks</a> explains why heat is the strongest argument against charging any battery in direct sun, and the same applies to a magnetic pack baking on a dashboard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Faster is not free. A 25W Qi2.2 bank pushes more power through the coils and makes more heat than a 15W bank, so it relies more on the phone and bank managing temperature well. Makers address this with cooling designs, such as ESR’s CryoBoost, and the phone will slow down if it needs to. If you do not need the speed, 15W is gentler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, store a bank partly charged if you will not use it for a while. Most makers recommend topping a stored bank up every few months, because a lithium cell left fully empty for a long time can drop below the level the protection circuit will recover. A bank you keep in a glovebox or an emergency kit is the one most likely to be found dead when you need it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="travel-outages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Flying, Outages and the Rest of Your Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every bank on this page is well inside the airline limit. US rules allow lithium power banks under 100Wh in carry-on luggage without approval; the Anker MagGo 10K is listed at 38.5Wh, and a 10,000mAh bank at the usual 3.7 to 3.85V works out to roughly 37 to 39Wh. Power banks are not allowed in checked bags, so keep yours in your carry-on. Belkin states that its 10K model is TSA carry-on compliant, and the same is true of the others by the numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            For outages, a magnetic bank is a good second line, not a first. It is excellent at keeping one phone alive for a day, and less good at keeping a household connected for three. If you are building a kit for storm season, read our <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">guide to portable chargers for power outages</a>, which sizes banks by how long you need to last. Pair it with a <a href="/best-hand-crank-radio/">hand crank radio</a> so you can still hear weather alerts when the cell network is overloaded, and an <a href="/best-emergency-lantern/">emergency lantern</a> so your phone flashlight is not your only light.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to recharge banks from the sun on a long trip or a long outage, skip banks with tiny built-in solar cells and pair a plain bank with a folding panel. Our <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">best solar power bank guide</a> explains why a folding panel beats built-in cells, and the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panel guide</a> covers larger panels if you are charging a power station.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical tip for travel: carry a short USB-C cable even if your bank has magnets. Airports, hotels and rental cars all have USB-C ports now, and a cable lets you refill the bank or charge the phone directly at full speed. If you buy the Baseus AM52 with its built-in cable, you have this covered already.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want one answer, buy the Anker MagGo Power Bank (10K). Full Qi2 speed, a stand you will use, a screen that tells the truth and a 27W USB-C port cover almost everything a magnetic bank can do. It is a bit heavy, and that is the only real criticism.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have an iPhone 16 or 17 and charge wirelessly most of the day, the Baseus PicoGo AM52 is the better buy. Qi2.2 at up to 25W, 45W wired output and a built-in cable make it the most capable bank here. If you have an older iPhone, its extra speed does nothing for you, and the Anker is the better value.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want something that lives on the back of your phone every day, buy the Anker Nano 5K MagGo Slim and accept that it is a top-up, not a full charge. If you charge several devices at once, the Belkin BoostCharge Pro 10K with its two USB-C ports is the one to get. If price is the deciding factor, the ESR HaloLock MagSlim gives you Qi2 and a kickstand for less than the big names.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, use a MagSafe-compatible case, plug in a cable when you can, and take the bank off once the phone is full. Those three habits make more difference to how long a magnetic bank lasts you each day than any spec on the box.
          </p>
        </div>

