        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best MagSafe power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most iPhone owners, the Anker MagGo Power Bank (10K). It is Qi2 certified for 15W magnetic charging, has a fold-out stand and a percentage display, and adds a 27W USB-C port. If you have an iPhone 16 or 17, the Baseus PicoGo AM52 is faster thanks to Qi2.2 at up to 25W, plus 45W USB-C and a built-in cable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Qi2 the same as MagSafe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not exactly, but close. MagSafe is Apple’s magnetic charging system. Qi2 is the open standard that adopted Apple’s magnetic alignment, so a Qi2-certified bank snaps onto a MagSafe iPhone and charges at up to 15W. Older “MagSafe compatible” banks without Qi2 usually charge iPhones at 7.5W or less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many times will a 10,000mAh MagSafe power bank charge my iPhone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About once for a current standard-size iPhone when charging wirelessly, a little more over a cable. Wireless charging loses a large share of the energy as heat; Baseus says about 5,500 to 6,500mAh of its 10,000mAh bank is usable. Larger Pro Max phones will get less than one full charge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do MagSafe power banks damage your battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in normal use, but heat is the thing to manage. Wireless charging warms both the phone and the bank, and iPhones slow charging when hot to protect the battery. Avoid charging in direct sun or a hot car, remove thick cases if the phone gets hot, and take the bank off once the phone is full.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take a magnetic power bank on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in carry-on luggage. US rules allow power banks under 100Wh in the cabin without approval, and a 10,000mAh bank is about 37 to 39Wh. Anker lists its MagGo 10K at 38.5Wh. Power banks are not allowed in checked bags, so keep yours with you.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do MagSafe power banks work with Android phones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many do if the phone has Qi2 magnets or you use a magnetic case. Some recent Android phones have Qi2 magnets built in. Without magnets the bank will still charge wirelessly if held in place, but it will not stay attached. Most Android phones charge at 15W or less even on a Qi2.2 bank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 25W Qi2.2 worth it over 15W Qi2?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if your phone supports it. The iPhone 17 line supports 25W Qi2.2, and Apple added it to most iPhone 16 models with iOS 26. Older iPhones and most Android phones still charge at 15W or less on a Qi2.2 bank, so for them a good 15W Qi2 bank is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
